feat: market search across online skill registries (M2)

The Market tab now searches the web for skills instead of reporting itself
not yet built. Two sources ship:

- skills.sh, the Agent Skills registry's search endpoint, which returns a
  skill's repository coordinates and an install count but neither a
  description nor a path;
- a GitHub source, which enumerates the skill directories of any repository
  the user names (a full URL or a .git suffix also work).

Selecting a result opens a detail dialog rendered by the shell's own
MarkdownText, listing the skill's files beside its body.

Two measured constraints shaped the layer:

- The unauthenticated GitHub API allows 60 requests/hour, so only discovery
  touches it and is cached for 30 minutes; file bodies come from raw
  content, which is outside that budget. Registry hits carry no ref, and
  HEAD turns out to work as a raw ref, so one search page spanning many
  repositories costs no API call at all.
- GitHub accelerator mirrors come and go (two of eight probed were dead), so
  raw fetches run through a degrading candidate chain that remembers the
  first node to answer; the API host is never routed through one, because
  mirrors generally do not pass API traffic and the budget is scarce.

Security: the browser only ever sends back the opaque skill id the host
issued. The id grammar is validated segment by segment when decoded, so a
path traversal or a foreign URL is rejected before it reaches any fetch.
A source that is down degrades the page - the other results still show and
the failure is noted once above them.

Engineering note: exporting the market class from lib/index.js was folded
away by the bundler (the re-export shares a binding with makeRoutes's
internal use), and a bridge const then referenced the bundle's renamed
local and crashed at import. The market modules are emitted under their own
companion entry (lib/market.js) instead, which the tests import.

Tests: 9 market checks (aggregation, de-duplication, ranking, the id grammar
against four path-escape attempts, the skills.sh contract) - all with a fake
transport, none touching the network.
